<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stelvio Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stilfser Joch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stelvio Pass is a mountain pass in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at 2,757 metres, one of the highest paved passes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinschgau">Vinschgau</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>. Until 1918 it lay on the border between Italy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and was fought over in 1866 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stelvio-pass.jpg" alt="Stelvio Pass" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stelvio_Pass_Bolzano_side_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Stelvio Pass, in Italian Passo dello Stelvio and in German Stilfser Joch, lies at 2,757 metres in the Ortler Alps of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. It runs between Stilfs, in Italian Stelvio, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormio">Bormio</a> in the province of Sondrio, and connects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinschgau">Vinschgau</a> valley and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Meran</a>. It is the highest paved mountain pass in the Eastern Alps. The Umbrail Pass joins its western ramp to Santa Maria Val Müstair. The nearby Three Languages Peak is named because the Italian, German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romansh">Romansh language</a> areas meet there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> built the original road in 1820-25 to link its former possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, a climb of 1,871 metres. Carlo Donegani was the engineer and project manager, and was knighted in 1840 as Carl Donegani von Stilfserberg. The route has changed very little since. The southern side is 22 kilometres long with 34 hairpin turns and six tunnels near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormio">Bormio</a>, and the northern side is 28 kilometres long with 48 mostly tight hairpin bends, numbered with stones.</p><p>Before the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the pass formed the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> and the Italian Kingdom. Switzerland had an outpost and a hotel on the Three Languages Peak, and fierce battles were fought in ice and snow around it. The three states agreed not to fire over Swiss territory, which jutted out between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to the south, so that they fired down the pass instead. After the war and the expansion of Italy the pass lost its strategic importance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The pass is open to traffic from May to November and is important to cycling and motorcycling. The Giro d'Italia first crossed it in 1953, when Fausto Coppi beat Hugo Koblet, and when it is the highest point of a race it is named the Cima Coppi. On one day each year, the Stelvio Bike Day, roads to the pass are closed to all but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a>. Stelvio National Park, established in 1935 and the largest in the Italian Alps, covers the Ortles-Cevedale massif, and its wildlife includes deer, chamois, ibex and wolves. A summer skiing area lies beside the pass road.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stelvio Pass” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Austro-Prussian War of 1866 this force, allied with Austria, was sent to guard the <b>Stelvio Pass</b> on the Tyrolean border against Italy; it saw no combat and, according to a well-known story, came home with one more man than it had set out with.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Military</a></li></ul></section>
